/** @page jni_use_java_in_qoreguide Using Java APIs in Qore

    @tableofcontents

    @section jniimport Importing Java APIs into Qore

    To import Java APIs into the %Qore program; any of the following jni-module-specific parse directives can be used,
    each of which also causes the jni module to be loaded and initialized:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) import java.namespace.path.* @endcode imports the given wilcard path or class into
      the %Qore program
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) add-classpath filesystem/path:another/path @endcode adds the given paths to the
      Program-specific runtime dynamic classpath; supports environment variable substitution
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) add-relative-classpath ../relative/path @endcode adds the given paths as relative
      to the current program to the Program-specific runtime dynamic classpath
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) global-add-classpath filesystem/path:another/path @endcode adds the given paths to the
      global runtime dynamic classpath; supports environment variable substitution
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) global-add-relative-classpath ../relative/path @endcode adds the given paths as relative
      to the current program to the global runtime dynamic classpath
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) register-shared-class qore.Namespace.ClassName @endcode gives an exact dynamically
      projected %Qore class one Java identity across sibling %Program classloaders; the first registering %Program
      owns the Java class and every later registrant delegates to it

    All classes in \c java.lang.* are imported implicitly.  Referencing an imported class in %Qore code
    causes a %Qore class to be generated dynamically that presents the Java class.   Instantiating a %Qore class
    based on a Java class also instantiates an internal Java object that is attached to the %Qore object.  Calling
    methods on the object will cause the same Java methods to be called with any arguments provided.

    An import is visible only in the @ref Qore::Program "Program" that made it, and in the child Programs that
    inherit that Program's classes when they are created.  Another Program that loads the jni module, whether it is
    created before or after the import, does not see the class until it imports the class itself; code must therefore
    import every Java class that it names outside \c java.lang.*, even if another Program has already imported it.

    @see @ref jni_class_mapping for more information

    @section jni_define_class_parse_time Defining Java Classes at Parse Time in Qore

    The jni module also supports defining classes at parse time with the following jni-module-specific parse
    directives:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) define-class <name> <base 64 bytecode> @endcode defines the given class with
      \c &lt;name&gt; as the Java internal name for the class (ex: \c my/package/MyClassName)
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) define-pending-class <name> <base 64 bytecode> @endcode adds the given class and
      bytecode as a pending class to be defined when required by a \c define-class directive; this should be used for
      inner classes and has been implemented to allow circular dependencies in such classes to be resolved;
      \c &lt;name&gt; is the Java internal name for the inner class (ex: \c my/package/MyClassName$1)

    @section jni_set_property_parse_time Setting Java System Properties at Parse Time in Qore

    The jni module also supports setting Java system properties at parse time with the following jni-module-specific
    parse directive:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) set-property <propname> <value> @endcode sets the given property at parse time

    @section jni_class_mapping Java Class and Package Mapping in Qore

    @subsection jni_package_mapping Java Package to Qore Namespace Mapping

    The \c jni module maps Java packages to %Qore namespaces.  Therefore in Java \c java.lang.String can be referred
    to as \c java::lang::String in Qore as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
    java::lang::String str();
    @endcode

    The \c jni module attempts to create %Qore classes that match the source Java class as closely as possible to
    maximize the usefulness of Java APIs in %Qore.

    When creating %Qore class methods from the corresponding Java source method, types are mapped according to
    @ref jni_java_to_qore.  Because %Qore has fewer types than Java, this can lead to method signature collissions in
    the generated %Qore class.

    In such cases, only the first method returned by the internal call to \c java.lang.Class.getDeclaredMethods() is
    mapped.

    To call an unmapped method, use reflection to get a \c java.lang.Method object and make the call using
    \c java.lang.Method.invoke() as in the folllowing example:
    @code{.py}
    StringBuilder stringBuilder("");
    Method stringBuilderAppendLong = stringBuilderClass.getMethod("append", Long::TYPE);
    stringBuilderAppendLong.invoke(stringBuilder, 20);
    printf("%y\n", stringBuilder.toString());
    @endcode

    @subsection jniclassmapping Java Inner Classes in Qore

    Java inner classes are mapped as %Qore classes by appending \c "__" to the outer class's name and then appending
    the name of the inner class.

    For example:
    @code{.java}
class Outer {
   public Outer() {
   }

   public class Inner {
       public Inner() {
       }
   }
}
    @endcode

    The inner class would be created as \c "Outer__Inner" in %Qore.

    @subsection jni_class_subclassing Subclassing Java Classes in Qore

    When subclassing Java classes in %Qore, the %Qore class code is not executed by Java when dispatching method calls
    in Java itself.  This means that overriding Java methods in %Qore is only valid for code executed from %Qore.

    To override a Java method when called from Java, you must subclass the class in Java as well and import the child
    class into your %Qore program.

    One exception to this limitation is with interfaces; programmers can use the
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" class to execute %Qore-language callbacks
    based on Java interfaces; see the documentation for
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" for more information.

    @subsection jni_callback_sandbox Qore Callbacks on Java Threads and Sandboxing

    Java can call %Qore code that it has been given - a
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" proxy, a closure or call reference
    passed to Java as an @ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosure, or a method of a %Qore object passed to Java - on any thread,
    including a Java thread that %Qore did not start (a Java executor, a listener thread of a messaging client, a
    timer thread).  Such a call runs under the sandbox (\c Qore::SandboxManager) of the code that handed the %Qore
    code to Java: the code that created the \c QoreInvocationHandler, the code that passed the closure or the object
    to Java (each Java wrapper of an object carries the sandbox of the code that created it), or, for an object that
    Java creates, the code that runs the Java constructor.  The release of the %Qore code, when the handler is
    destroyed or Java no longer references the closure, and the destruction of an object that Java destroys, run
    under the same sandbox.  A callback can therefore never do more than the code that registered it, whatever
    thread Java calls it on.

    @subsection jni_sandbox Sandboxing Java I/O

    %Qore enforces the network and filesystem policy of a sandbox (\c Qore::SandboxManager, ex:
    \c SandboxManager::blockPrivateNetworks()) for the I/O that goes through %Qore: sockets, QUIC and file
    operations.  Java code called through this module does its own I/O through the JVM - \c java.net, \c java.nio,
    \c java.io, JDBC drivers and other Java libraries - which %Qore never sees, and the JDK has no in-process
    replacement for the Security Manager (deprecated by JEP 411 and disabled by JEP 486).  A sandboxed caller can
    therefore reach a private network or the filesystem through any Java code path that does not check its I/O
    itself.  Java classes imported through this module are tagged with the \c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API functional
    domain for this reason, so that a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS cannot use them directly.

    Java code that sandboxed %Qore code may call - for example the actions of a data provider implemented in Java,
    which connect to destinations taken from a tenant's connection - checks every network destination and every
    path it uses with @ref org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox "org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox", which applies the policy of the
    %Qore code that called into Java:
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.checkNetworkAccess(host, port)</tt> resolves the host name once with the %Qore resolver and
      checks every address with the network policy of %Qore sockets (denied ranges, allowed host patterns, ports and
      ranges, the default policy); it raises \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED naming the denied address, and returns the
      checked addresses as \c java.net.InetAddress objects that carry the host name; the Java code connects to one of
      them, never to the host name, so that a second lookup cannot return an address that was not checked (DNS
      rebinding)
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.checkFileAccess(path, write)</tt> and <tt>QoreSandbox.checkFileAccess(path, mode)</tt> check
      a path with the filesystem policy of %Qore \c File and \c Dir operations, and raise
      \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.isSandboxed()</tt> tells whether a sandbox governs the caller

    The exceptions are thrown in Java as \c org.qore.jni.QoreExceptionWrapper (a \c RuntimeException), which becomes
    the original %Qore exception when it reaches %Qore code.  Trusted callers pass every check; the host name is
    resolved for them as well, so the code is the same in both cases.  In a system-policy capability
    (\c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()) the checks apply the system policy, as they do for %Qore sockets.

    <b>Java threads:</b> the static methods apply the policy of the %Qore caller of the current thread.  Work that
    Java runs on threads of its own (an executor, a connection pool, the I/O threads of a client library) has no
    %Qore caller: Java code captures the policy with <tt>QoreSandbox.capture()</tt> in the call from %Qore, before
    the work is handed over, and makes the checks through the returned \c QoreSandbox.Policy object, or runs the work
    with <tt>Policy.run()</tt>, <tt>Policy.call()</tt> or <tt>Policy.wrap()</tt>, which make the static methods use
    the captured policy on that thread.  A check made on a thread with no %Qore caller and no captured policy raises
    \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR instead of allowing the operation, so that a missing capture is an error and never a silent
    bypass.  %Qore code that Java calls back on a Java thread already runs under the sandbox of the code that handed
    it to Java (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox), so the checks it makes through Java use that sandbox.

    A captured \c QoreSandbox.Policy applies to every check made through it on any thread, also on a %Qore thread that
    runs other sandboxed code and inside a system-policy capability (\c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall());
    the policy of the %Qore code that runs on the thread applies as well, so that the stricter one wins.

    @par Example:
    @code{.java}
import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox;

public class Fetcher {
    // called from Qore
    public static void fetch(ExecutorService executor, String host, int port) {
        // captured on the calling thread: the policy of the Qore code that called fetch()
        QoreSandbox.Policy policy = QoreSandbox.capture();
        executor.submit(policy.wrap(() -> {
            try (QoreSandbox.Policy p = policy; Socket s = new Socket()) {
                // on the executor thread: checked with the captured policy
                InetAddress[] addrs = QoreSandbox.checkNetworkAccess(host, port);
                // connect to the checked address, not to the host name
                s.connect(new InetSocketAddress(addrs[0], port));
                // ...
            }
            return null;
        }));
    }
}
    @endcode

    <b>Sandbox-aware Java code:</b> Java code may be declared to be sandbox-aware - safe to call from sandboxed
    %Qore code - only if it has been audited to follow these rules:
    - every network destination is checked with \c checkNetworkAccess() before it is used, and the connection is made
      to a returned address; a library that connects by host name (and resolves it again) or that connects to
      destinations it discovers itself (ex: the brokers that a Kafka bootstrap server returns, or redirects followed
      by an HTTP client) is configured not to, or each such destination is checked as well
    - every path is checked with \c checkFileAccess() with the access mode of the operation, as passed to the
      operation, before the file or directory is used
    - work run on other threads is checked with a policy captured in the call from %Qore
    - no I/O is made before the check; an operation that cannot be checked is not made

    A Java-backed data provider or module that has not been audited is not sandbox-aware, and must not be made
    available to sandboxed callers.  A data provider app declares this in its \c DataProviderAppInfo (\c DataProvider
    3.12 or later): \c external_io when its actions do I/O that the sandbox does not see, and \c sandbox_aware
    only after the audit; data provider consumers refuse sandboxed callers an app with \c external_io and without
    \c sandbox_aware (\c DataProviderActionCatalog::checkSandboxAccess(), \c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED).

    <b>Data provider apps of this module:</b>
    |!App|!Declaration|!The Java I/O that the sandbox does not see
    |Access|\c external_io|Jackcess opens the database file named by the action's path
    |Busylight, MQTT|\c external_io|the Paho client connects to the broker and to its failover URIs
    |Camel|\c external_io|Camel endpoints connect to and open what their URIs name
    |Flyway|\c external_io|the JDBC URL and the filesystem migration locations
    |JakartaJMS|\c external_io|the provider connects to the broker URL and loads the provider classpath
    |JasperReports|\c external_io|a report can load subreports, images and data from files and URLs
    |JdbcDP|\c external_io|the JDBC driver connects to the destinations of the URL
    |Kafka|\c external_io|the client connects to the bootstrap servers and to the brokers they return
    |OpcUA|\c external_io|the client connects to the server and the endpoints it returns
    |Avro, Email, Excel, ICS, ODP, ODS, ODT, PowerPoint, Tika, VCF, Visio, Word|none|Java only transforms the data

    No app is declared \c sandbox_aware yet: each app with \c external_io lets a Java library connect to destinations
    that it resolves or discovers itself, or open files it finds itself, which these checks cannot cover until the
    app is changed to make them.  The Excel and ODS apps create temporary files of their own in the JVM's temporary
    directory, with names that no caller chooses; these are not declared as external I/O.

    @subsection jni_fork Child Processes Created with fork()

    Java cannot be used in a child process created with \c fork() after the JVM has been started (when the module
    is loaded): only the thread that called \c fork() exists in the child, while the threads of the JVM (its
    garbage collectors and compilers, and the module's own threads) remain in the parent.  In such a child:
    - every use of Java, including a Java object created in the parent, raises a \c JNI-FORK-ERROR exception
    - the deletion of a %Qore object or Program that uses Java, and the module's cleanup when the child exits or
      ends its script, neither call Java nor wait for the threads of the JVM
    - %Qore code that Java called and that called \c fork() must end the child with \c exit(): the child cannot
      return to the Java code that called it

    A child process that needs Java must start a new process (ex: by calling \c exec() in the child).

    @subsection jni_class_fields Java Class Fields to Qore Class Mappings

    Java fields are mapped to different %Qore class members according to the Java type according to the following
    table.

    <b>Java Field to Qore Class Member Mappings</b>
    |!Java|!Qore|!Qore Example
    |<tt><b>static final</b></tt>|%Qore class constants|@code{.py}MyClass::myField@endcode
    |<tt><b>static</b></tt> (non-final)|%Qore static class members|@code{.py}obj.myField@endcode
    |all others|normal %Qore class members|@code{.py}obj.myField@endcode

    For example:
    @code{.py}
System::out.println("testing...");
    @endcode

    @note Java field values are only stored in Java; they are not copied into the %Qore object.  Reading a field's
    member reads the field of the Java object, and assigning it (including compound assignments such as
    <tt>obj.count += 1</tt>) writes the field of the Java object, whether the object was created with a %Qore
    constructor or in Java; removing or deleting the member sets the field to \c null (or zero for a primitive
    field).  A field's member is not returned by the object's keys or hash conversions.  An access from outside the
    class to a field that %Qore code cannot access goes to the class's \c memberGate() method.

    A field's member, or the static class member of a mutable static field, is declared with the %Qore type of the
    field's Java type (see @ref jni_declared_value_types "Declared Types of Java Values"), and the value read from the
    field is checked against it.  A field of a reference type (an object or an array) can be \c null, so its type
    accepts no value: a \c java.lang.String field is declared as \c *string, a \c java.lang.Integer field as \c *int,
    and an \c int[] field as <tt>*softlist&lt;int&gt;</tt>; a \c null field is read as no value, and assigning no
    value sets the field to \c null.  A field of a primitive type cannot be \c null, so its type does not accept no
    value.  A \c java.lang.Character field is declared as \c *int, as a \c Character is converted to an integer, and a
    field of a class that the classes of values converted to %Qore values implement or extend (ex:
    \c java.lang.Number, \c java.lang.CharSequence, \c java.lang.Comparable, \c java.time.temporal.Temporal,
    \c java.util.Date) is declared as \c auto, as a value of such a class (ex: an \c Integer in a \c Number field) is
    converted to the %Qore value of its own class.

    A \c static \c final field is mirrored by a class constant holding the field's value, whose type is the type of
    the value; a \c null field is a constant with no value, declared with the field's type (ex: \c *string for a
    \c java.lang.String field).

    @subsection jni_access_mapping Java Access Mapping in Qore

    Java access modifiers are mapped directly to Qore access modifiers as in the following table.

    <b>Java to Qore Access Mappings</b>
    |!Java|!Qore|!Declared types
    |<b><tt>public</tt></b>|<b><tt>public</tt></b>|the %Qore types of the Java types
    |<b><tt>protected</tt></b>|<b><tt>private</tt></b> (or <b><tt>private:hierarchy</tt></b>)|the %Qore types of \
        the Java types
    |package-private (no modifier)|<b><tt>public</tt></b> (see @ref jni_package_private)|the %Qore types of the \
        Java types
    |<b><tt>private</tt></b>|<b><tt>private:internal</tt></b>|\c auto (see @ref jni_member_types)

    @subsection jni_package_private Package-Private Java Members

    A package-private Java member, one declared without an access modifier, is \c public in %Qore.  Calling it works
    when Java allows reflective access to it:
    - always for a class loaded from the classpath, as all its packages are open
    - for a class in a named module, such as the JDK's own modules, only if the module opens the class's package to
      the code of the \c jni module; otherwise the call raises a \c java.lang.IllegalAccessException.  A package is
      opened when the JVM starts, for example with
      <tt>QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS=--add-opens=java.base/java.util=ALL-UNNAMED</tt>, or while it runs, by the module itself
      (\c Module.addOpens()) or by a Java agent.

    As this access can change while the JVM runs, a package-private member is always \c public in %Qore, whether or
    not it can be called when its class is imported.

    @subsection jni_member_types Declared Types of Java Members

    The %Qore class created for a Java class declares the types of its members' parameters, return values and fields
    with the %Qore classes created for the Java classes they name, which are imported with it.  A Java class that
    cannot be loaded or described there, typically because it needs an optional dependency that is not in the
    classpath, is declared as \c auto; this is reported once per process on \c stderr, naming the class and the
    Java exception, because a %Qore class built with such a type does not match the same class built where the
    dependency is available.

    The types of return values, fields and constants follow the rules in @ref jni_declared_value_types "Declared Types
    of Java Values": a value of a reference type can be \c null, so its type accepts no value (ex: \c *string for a
    \c java.lang.String return value).  The types of parameters follow the rules in @ref jni_declared_param_types
    "Declared Types of Java Parameters": a %Qore argument selects the variant Java would select for the value it is
    converted to.

    The parameter, return value and field types of \c private Java members are always declared as \c auto, and the
    classes they name are not imported for them: a private member cannot be called from %Qore code, only through the
    \c reflection module (ex: \c NormalMethod::call()), where Java allows reflective access to it (always for
    classes loaded from the classpath).  So an optional dependency that a library uses only
    internally is not reported either (for example the SQL Server JDBC driver's antlr dependency, needed only for
    its \c useFmtOnly option).  The \c reflection module therefore shows the variants of private Java methods and
    constructors, and private fields, with \c auto types; their arguments are converted to the Java parameter types
    when they are called.  A private Java method whose %Qore signature with \c auto types is the same as that of a
    non-private method of the same name is not added to the %Qore class.  Private \c static \c final fields are
    still constants with their values.

    @section jni_classloader Java Classloader

    There is a global classloader for all Java objects reachable without special classpath.  For all other objects,
    each %Qore @ref Qore::Program "Program" container has its own custom classloader provided by the \c jni module
    that supports a dynamic classpath.

    The %Qore @ref Qore::Program "Program"-specific dynamic classpath can be set with the \c import commands as
    documented in @ref jniimport.

    Explicit class loaders can also be used as in Java as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
    softlist<URL> urls = (new URL("file:///my/dir/my-api.jar"));
    URLClassLoader classLoader(urls);
    Class myClass = classLoader.loadClass("MyClass");
    @endcode

    @note The class loader supplied by this module, @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader, supports dynamic bytecode
    generation from Python and %Qore imports.

    Same-path classes projected as \c qore.<i>Namespace</i>.<i>Class</i> are Program-local by default because separate
    %Programs can legitimately provide different implementations at the same path.  Applications that pass objects
    of one semantically identical %Qore API class between sibling Program classloaders must register that exact Java
    binary name before loading it in any participating Program:
    @code{.py}
%module-cmd(jni) register-shared-class qore.MyApi.Request
    @endcode
    The first registering Program becomes the process-wide owner for that name, and registration by other Programs is
    idempotent.  Registering after two loaders have already defined the name raises an error because an existing JVM
    class-identity split cannot be repaired.  Do not register a name whose Qore definitions differ between Programs.

    @section jni_exceptions Java Exceptions in Qore

    If a @ref org.qore.jni.QoreException exception is thrown, then it is mapped directly to a Qore exception.

    Other Java exceptions are mapped to the %Qore @ref Qore::ExceptionInfo "ExceptionInfo" hash as follows:
    - \c err: always \c "JNI-ERROR"
    - \c desc: the full Java class name of the exception class; ex: \c "java.lang.RuntimeException"
    - \c arg: the exception object itself as a %Qore object wrapping the Java object

    Exception locations including call stack locations reflect the actual Java source location(s), and in such cases
    the \c lang attribute will be \c "Java". Negative Java source line numbers, including the native-method
    sentinel \c -2, are exposed as Qore's unknown-location sentinel \c -1. Available source line numbers are preserved.
*/
